@Article(time: 4) {
  @Intro(title: "Setup") {
    Only `DATABASE_URL` is required, and every other name reads as its default. A name the
    configuration does not read answers nothing, so a typo in a name here is not an error the run
    reports; that is why the list below is the whole set the runs read.

    ```sh
    export DATABASE_URL=/var/lib/rivulet/rivulet.db
    export LISTEN_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8080
    export BASE_URL=https://reader.example.com
    export RUN_MIGRATIONS=1
    export POLLING_FREQUENCY=60
    export POLLING_PARSING_ERROR_LIMIT=3
    export LOG_LEVEL=info
    export LOG_FORMAT=text
    export FETCHER_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORKS=0
    ```

    `LISTEN_ADDR` is read as a host with a port, so the port comes from the value as well and the
    default is the loopback listener `127.0.0.1:8080`. A value with no port is reported as an
    invalid value rather than read as the default.

    Each integer read carries its own minimum, and the number inside parentheses below is the number
    that read takes: `POLLING_FREQUENCY` (1), `BATCH_SIZE` (1), `WORKER_POOL_SIZE` (1), and the two
    count reads take (0). A value below the number the read takes is reported rather than read as
    the fallback, and a name the source reads with a trim alone is read as free text.

    One name is the address policy: on a host that can reach its own addresses it stays off, and the
    fetcher refuses a target in a class the policy names. A feed that lives on the host's own
    network needs it on, which is a decision one run makes rather than a default.

    Two commands read their values from the terminal: the account command and the credential
    command. The secure read the dependency uses takes its values from the terminal device, so these
    two run on a console and not from a unit or a pipe, where the same read answered nothing.

    The commands the pages here call are the commands the dispatch registers, and the flags they use
    are the flags those signatures declare, which is what the check half reads them against.
  }
}
